am to E001.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no\n  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no\n  Non-Functional Requirements section. The decomposition proceeds from the\n  HLR table, Affected Components list, and problem statement. Team assignment\n  for the documentation epic is an assumption: with no Impacted Teams table,\n  the docs-authoring epic is assigned to RHOAI Dashboard (the team that owns\n  the fixes and must provide the technical review); reassign if a dedicated\n  documentation team owns the accessibility statement.\n- **Epic size overage kept deliberately**: E001 covers keyboard/focus\n  remediation across all four audited flows in one epic. It is kept as one\n  epic because it is one logical change (audit-enumerated keyboard and focus\n  defect remediation of the same kind on the same console) for one component\n  and team; a split would fragment a single mechanical pass over shared focus\n  management surfaces. **Re-split trigger**: re-split along flow boundaries\n  (one epic per audited flow, or code-flow fixes vs. shared focus-indicator\n  work) if the audit's enumerated defect list is materially larger than the\n  estimate or if any single flow's remediation exceeds roughly one week of\n  effort, so that review cycles can proceed in parallel.\n- **Passive validation note**: no passive components — both affected\n  components have deliverables in this strategy.\n- **External commitment risk (not a DAG dependency)**: the published VPAT is\n  required by two public-sector customers before renewal. The renewal deadline\n  is a business constraint on scheduling, not an epic dependency; it is\n  recorded here for planning awareness.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable — no external dependency with uncertain timing. Both epics are\ninternally scheduled; the documentation epic simply follows the code work.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":nu